    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i've noticed the shards from this specific stratum are really patchy though. like, some parts are bright red and the other parts are almost black, even on the same piece. i'm worried it's not a consistent firing technique but just random variations. what would cause that kind of unevenness in the coloring?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Patchy coloration on a single shard, like bright red next to near-black, is a classic sign of inconsistent kiln conditions, which is far more indicative of a chaotic firing than a single, deliberate technique. This unevenness is often caused by localized variations in the oxygen supply within the kiln, which is a real-world problem for any ancient potter.\n\nThink of it like a poorly managed batch of ingredients in a large, smoky oven—some parts get enough air to turn red, while others get choked off and turn dark. In the context of your trench, this points toward either a poorly sealed kiln or uneven airflow across the firing chamber.\n\nSometimes, it can also be related to the clay itself—if the clay body has mineral inclusions of different compositions, they might react differently to the fluctuating oxygen levels. However, the dramatic shift from red to black usually points back to the atmosphere.\n\nIt's a fascinating mess, but it tells you more about the *process* of ancient pottery making than a single, perfect piece ever could! You might need to look at the distribution of these patches across multiple fragments from that stratum.",
      "source": "target",
